// std/file.jtr — STREAMING file IO, and the first module that reaches the
// operating system without a compiler intrinsic.
//
// ─── Why this module is the interesting one ───────────────────────────────────
//
// Every other IO path in the tree slurps: `read_file`/`try_read_file` hand back
// the whole file as a `String`. `std/cursor` is a reader over memory, which is
// honest but is not streaming. The roadmap recorded the fix as a new *intrinsic*
// — a file handle plus `read_into(handle, buf)`, paying the eleven-site recipe
// and a reseed, and introducing a resource with a lifetime into the compiler.
//
// None of that was needed. `fread` already exists, and what was missing was only
// a way to SPELL its arguments. Two small language facts close the gap:
//
//   1. `cptr` — C's `void*`, an opaque handle. It holds the `FILE*` without
//      Jestyr having to know what a `FILE` is.
//   2. `extern "stdio.h"` — the string after `extern` says where the declaration
//      comes from. `"c"` means "nowhere, emit one"; a header name means "that
//      header already did, so do NOT emit one".
//
// The second is the one that actually unblocked this, and the roadmap's diagnosis
// was wrong about it. `void*` alone does not work: C requires a redeclaration to
// be *compatible*, and `int fclose(void*)` is not compatible with
// `int fclose(FILE*)` — implicit `void*` conversion applies to VALUES, not to
// prototypes. So the fix is to not redeclare at all, and then `cptr` values pass
// through `<stdio.h>`'s own declarations by exactly those implicit conversions.
//
// ─── What this is NOT ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// There is deliberately no `Reader` TRAIT here. That is still the open design
// question `docs/io-design.md` records — four decisions, none of which this module
// answers — and shipping a trait to have one would be the "iterators in a library
// costume" mistake. `Reader` here is a concrete type that streams; when the trait
// question is settled, this is what it will be settled against.
//
// Nor is there an error set ON THE READ HALF. `fread` reports a short count and
// `ferror` reports why, which is two calls, not fifty `?`s down a formatter — the
// same argument `io-design.md` makes for putting the error set at the end and
// nowhere else. The write half has exactly one fallible call, `finish`, for that
// same reason and no other; see below.
//
// ─── Writing: one fallible call, and it is not `flush` ────────────────────────
//
// `io-design.md` §3 named the right shape — many infallible writes, one fallible
// operation at the end — and named the wrong call. It said `flush`.
//
// **The first version of this comment justified that with a measurement that proved
// nothing, and the honest record is worth more than the tidy claim.** It said "over a
// stream that has already lost bytes, `fflush` returns 0". The probe behind it opened
// a file `"rb"`, watched `fwrite` refuse every byte, and then flushed — but because
// the write delivered nothing, the output buffer was EMPTY, so the 0 meant "an empty
// buffer flushed cleanly", which is true of every conforming implementation including
// one that reports losses faithfully. Deleting the write from the probe left the
// assertion passing. Worse, `fflush` on an input stream is undefined behaviour
// (C11 7.21.5.2) — in a module whose scope rule is "if ISO C specifies it".
//
// The conclusion survives on a structural argument that needs no measurement:
// **`fclose` performs the final implicit flush and reports it**, so the close is
// genuinely last and subsumes anything a `flush` could say. Shipping both would give a
// caller two places to ask one question, and the earlier one cannot answer it — that
// is the always-answers-fine shape `writer.jtr` already refused once, and it does not
// need a benchmark to reject.
//
// So `finish` is the one fallible function here, `flush` does not exist, and a
// formatter emitting fifty pieces carries zero `?`s: fifty `write_str` calls and one
// `finish`.
//
// `flush` becomes worth adding when a caller must learn about durability MID-stream
// without ending the file — a log tailed by another process. Nothing in the tree has
// one, and a `flush` added before that caller exists would be a second, weaker place
// to ask the question `finish` answers.
//
// ─── What `Drop` on a `Writer` does and does not promise ──────────────────────
//
// `finish` is where a write is REPORTED. `Drop` is where the descriptor is
// RECLAIMED. Letting a `Writer` fall out of scope is choosing not to know: bytes
// that `fwrite` accepted into stdio's buffer and that the Drop-time `fclose` then
// failed to push are counted by `written()`, invisible to everything, and lost after
// the last Jestyr expression in the scope.
//
// That is stated plainly rather than hedged, because `Drop` CANNOT report and
// nothing here will make it able to: a fallible trait-impl method is refused by name,
// there is no return channel, a module-level "last error" latch is the errno design
// and a data race in a language with real `spawn`, and `eprint_str` from a library
// writes to a stream the caller may be formatting into in order to complain about
// output. There is no fifth channel.
//
// The mitigation that is real: `is_writable(w)` is false IFF you finished, so "did I
// remember?" is answerable before scope exit, by a caller and by a test. The real fix
// is a language feature — a must-use obligation making an un-`finish`ed `Writer` at
// scope exit a compile error. It is not merely nice to have: a discarded fallible
// result in statement position compiles and runs today with no diagnostic, so even
// `file.finish(w)` written as a statement throws the verdict away silently. Nothing
// in this module makes that hard to do by accident, and this comment will not pretend
// otherwise.
//
// Two more limits, named here rather than discovered later. A `Drop`-owning struct is
// freely copy-initializable and the source stays readable, so `var b: Writer = a`
// leaves two names for one handle — the same missing linear-type obligation. And ISO
// C cannot close the false-ok case: if a platform's `fclose` returns 0 without ever
// setting the error indicator while bytes were dropped downstream, `finish` returns
// ok and is wrong. `fsync` is POSIX, and by this module's own scope rule that makes
// it `sys`'s.
//
// ─── Opening a computed path ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// `open` takes a `cstr`, because that is what `fopen` takes, and `.cstr` bridges
// a string LITERAL only. `open_path` is the general entry: it takes a `read str`,
// copies it through `std/cstring` into a NUL-terminated buffer, and frees that
// buffer before returning — so the terminator's lifetime never escapes this
// module and callers never hold a `cstr` at all. `create`/`create_path` and
// `append`/`append_path` are the same pair on the write side, sharing one private
// opener; the refusal matters MORE there, because a truncated read path opens the
// wrong file while a truncated write path clobbers it.
//
// It also REFUSES a path containing an interior NUL, rather than letting C stop
// early at it. `open_path("a\0/../etc/passwd")` silently opening `a` would be a
// security answer dressed as a correctness one; a closed `Reader` is the honest
// one. `cstring.has_interior_nul` is public for callers who want to distinguish
// that refusal from a missing file.
//
// ─── What belongs here, and what `sys` is for ─────────────────────────────────
//
// With `cptr` and header-declared externs, binding an OS call is no longer hard —
// which makes SCOPE the question, not capability. The rule this module follows:
//
//   **If ISO C specifies it, it can live in `std`. If it needs a platform, it
//   needs a platform layer, and that is what `sys` is for.**
//
// `fopen`/`fread`/`fclose`/`feof`/`ferror`/`fseek`/`ftell`/`rewind` are all ISO C:
// one binding behaves the same everywhere, so there is nothing for a platform tier
// to abstract and putting them behind one would be ceremony.
//
// Directory listing is the clean counter-example, and it is why `sys` still has
// work to do: POSIX spells it `opendir`/`readdir`/`closedir`, Windows spells it
// `FindFirstFile`/`FindNextFile`, and the two do not merely differ in name — they
// differ in what a directory entry *is*. That is a real abstraction with a real
// design cost, and it cannot be smuggled in as "just another extern". The same
// goes for file metadata (`stat` vs `GetFileAttributesEx`), permissions, symlinks
// and process control.
//
// So the boundary is not "OS calls go in `sys`" — this module makes OS calls. It
// is "**calls that need a portability decision go in `sys`**", and everything here
// needs none.
//
// Nothing here is `@no_os`: this module IS the OS boundary, and it is the first
// file in the tree where that is the point rather than a failure.
//
// Run the tests:  jestyrc test examples/std/file_test.jtr

// `mem` for the `Drop` trait (the same reason `std/pathbuf` imports it) and for
// the allocator `open_path` hands to `cstring`.
import "mem"
import "cstring"

// The stdio family. Declared by <stdio.h>, which the prelude already includes —
// hence the header name rather than "c". Emitting prototypes for these is a
// compile ERROR, not a redundancy; see `cgen::extern_protos`.
extern "stdio.h" fn fopen(path: cstr, mode: cstr) -> cptr
extern "stdio.h" fn fclose(f: cptr) -> i32
extern "stdio.h" fn fread(buf: cptr, size: usize, n: usize, f: cptr) -> usize
extern "stdio.h" fn fwrite(buf: cptr, size: usize, n: usize, f: cptr) -> usize
extern "stdio.h" fn feof(f: cptr) -> i32
extern "stdio.h" fn ferror(f: cptr) -> i32
// Seeking. `fseek`/`ftell`/`rewind` are ISO C, so they behave the same on every
// platform Jestyr targets — which is the rule that decides what belongs here at
// all (see the header's note on scope).
extern "stdio.h" fn fseek(f: cptr, off: i64, whence: i32) -> i32
extern "stdio.h" fn ftell(f: cptr) -> i64
extern "stdio.h" fn rewind(f: cptr)

// `SEEK_END`/`SEEK_SET` are macros, so their VALUES are what crosses the boundary.
// 0/1/2 are fixed by ISO C and by every ABI Jestyr targets; `size` below is pinned
// against an independent byte count in the test, so a wrong constant here fails
// loudly rather than returning a plausible number.
const SEEK_SET: i32 = 0
const SEEK_END: i32 = 2

// An open file, read sequentially.
//
// The handle is `cptr` rather than `*mut u8` for two reasons, and the second is
// the one that matters: a `cptr` cannot be dereferenced and cannot have arithmetic
// done to it, because it is not a pointer type in Jestyr's grammar at all. So this
// struct cannot be made to read the FILE's innards even inside `unsafe` — there is
// no operation to write. A `*mut u8` would have offered several.
pub struct Reader {
    h: cptr,
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ opening

// Open `path` for binary reading. Check `is_open` before reading: a failed open
// is reported as a closed `Reader`, not as an error value, because there is
// exactly one failure and a whole error set for it would be ceremony.
//
// Binary mode ("rb") is not a default, it is a decision: text mode on Windows
// rewrites `\r\n` to `\n` on the way past, so the SAME file would yield different
// bytes on different platforms. A determinism-first language does not get to do
// that silently. Callers wanting line endings normalised should normalise them.
pub fn open(path: cstr) -> Reader {
    return Reader{ h: fopen(path, "rb".cstr) }
}

// Open a path computed at runtime — the general entry; `open` is the literal-only
// shorthand.
//
// Returns a CLOSED reader for a path containing an interior NUL, because C would
// otherwise stop at it and open a different, shorter path than the caller named.
// That is indistinguishable from "no such file" here on purpose: both mean "you
// did not get what you asked for", and a caller who needs to tell them apart can
// ask `cstring.has_interior_nul` before calling.
//
// The NUL-terminated copy is freed before this returns, so no `cstr` outlives the
// call and the caller never sees one.
pub fn open_path(read a: Allocator, read p: str) -> Reader {
    if cstring.has_interior_nul(p) { return Reader{ h: null } }
    var c: cstring.CString = cstring.from_str(a, p)
    var r: Reader = Reader{ h: fopen(cstring.as_cstr(c), "rb".cstr) }
    cstring.free(c)
    return r
}

// Did the open succeed? A `Reader` for which this is false is inert: every read
// returns 0 and `close` does nothing.
pub fn is_open(read r: Reader) -> bool {
    return r.h != null
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ reading

// Fill as much of `buf` as the file has left, returning the byte count.
//
// A short count means EOF **or** an error, and this deliberately does not
// collapse the two: ask `at_eof` or `failed`. Returning a sentinel would make the
// common loop (`for n > 0`) wrong on the uncommon path, which is how "read errors
// look like EOF" bugs are written.
pub fn read_into(mut r: Reader, mut buf: []u8) -> usize {
    if r.h == null { return 0 }
    if buf.len == 0 { return 0 }
    return fread(buf.ptr, 1, buf.len, r.h)
}

// Has the file been read to its end? Only meaningful after a read that returned
// less than the buffer size — `feof` reports a *past* attempt, not a prediction,
// which is C's semantics and not worth papering over.
pub fn at_eof(read r: Reader) -> bool {
    if r.h == null { return true }
    return feof(r.h) != 0
}

// Did a read fail? Distinct from `at_eof`: both make a read return short, and only
// this one means the bytes are missing rather than absent.
pub fn failed(read r: Reader) -> bool {
    if r.h == null { return false }
    return ferror(r.h) != 0
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ position

// The file's total size in bytes, or -1 if it cannot be determined.
//
// **This does not disturb the read position** — it saves it, measures, and
// restores. A `size` that silently rewound would make the obvious
// `let n = size(r)` inside a read loop skip or repeat data, and that bug is
// invisible until the file is bigger than one buffer.
//
// -1 rather than 0 for failure, because 0 is a real answer: an empty file exists
// and is legitimately zero bytes.
pub fn size(mut r: Reader) -> i64 {
    if r.h == null { return 0 - 1 }
    let here: i64 = ftell(r.h)
    if here < 0 { return 0 - 1 }
    if fseek(r.h, 0, SEEK_END) != 0 { return 0 - 1 }
    let n: i64 = ftell(r.h)
    if fseek(r.h, here, SEEK_SET) != 0 { return 0 - 1 }
    return n
}

// The current read offset, or -1 if unknown.
pub fn offset(read r: Reader) -> i64 {
    if r.h == null { return 0 - 1 }
    return ftell(r.h)
}

// Return to the start, clearing EOF. The one seek worth exposing before a general
// `seek(whence, off)`: re-reading from the beginning is the common need, and it
// cannot be spelled with the read API alone.
pub fn restart(mut r: Reader) {
    if r.h != null { rewind(r.h) }
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ teardown

// Close the file early. Idempotent, and composes with the automatic drop — the
// `std/list` contract: it clears the handle, so the `Drop` impl finds nothing left
// to do and a double close cannot reach `fclose` twice.
pub fn close(mut r: Reader) {
    if r.h != null {
        fclose(r.h)
        r.h = null
    }
}

impl Drop for Reader {
    fn drop(mut self) {
        if self.h != null { fclose(self.h) }
    }
}

// ==================================================================== writing
//
// An open file, written sequentially.
//
// `put` counts bytes the STREAM ACCEPTED — not bytes on disk. Nothing short of
// `finish` can say whether they landed, and the field exists because `finish` needs
// a value to hand back; `written()` is that value made readable for free rather than
// a second, weaker way to ask about failure.
//
// `cptr` for the handle for `Reader`'s recorded reason: it cannot be dereferenced and
// has no arithmetic, not even inside `unsafe`, so this struct cannot be made to read
// the FILE's innards. `usize` for the counter to match `fwrite`, `read_into` and
// `finish`'s ok type, so no width conversion appears anywhere in the path.
pub struct Writer {
    h: cptr,
    put: usize,
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ opening

// Truncate `path` (or create it) and open it for binary writing.
//
// The mode is in the NAME rather than a parameter, because which of truncate and
// append you meant is a call-site decision and a `bool` at the call site does not
// say which way round it goes. Binary mode is the same determinism decision `open`
// made with "rb" and with more force: text mode EXPANDS `\n` on the way out, so the
// same program would write different bytes on different platforms.
pub fn create(path: cstr) -> Writer {
    return Writer{ h: fopen(path, "wb".cstr), put: 0 }
}

// Open `path` for binary writing, positioned at the end — existing bytes are kept.
pub fn append(path: cstr) -> Writer {
    return Writer{ h: fopen(path, "ab".cstr), put: 0 }
}

// `create` for a path computed at runtime.
pub fn create_path(read a: Allocator, read p: str) -> Writer {
    return open_for_write(a, p, "wb".cstr)
}

// `append` for a path computed at runtime.
pub fn append_path(read a: Allocator, read p: str) -> Writer {
    return open_for_write(a, p, "ab".cstr)
}

// The one place a computed write path is opened, so the interior-NUL refusal cannot
// be present in one entry point and forgotten in the other. Private because the mode
// belongs in the name; see `create`.
//
// Refusing an interior NUL is load-bearing here in a way it is not on the read side:
// C stopping early at the zero would not merely open the wrong file, it would
// TRUNCATE it.
fn open_for_write(read a: Allocator, read p: str, mode: cstr) -> Writer {
    if cstring.has_interior_nul(p) { return Writer{ h: null, put: 0 } }
    var c: cstring.CString = cstring.from_str(a, p)
    var w: Writer = Writer{ h: fopen(cstring.as_cstr(c), mode), put: 0 }
    cstring.free(c)
    return w
}

// Is there a file behind this handle? False for a failed open and false after
// `finish`, which is what makes it answerable as "did I remember to finish?" before
// the scope ends. A `Writer` for which this is false is inert: every write takes
// nothing and `Drop` does nothing.
pub fn is_writable(read w: Writer) -> bool {
    return w.h != null
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ writing

// Hand `buf` to the stream, returning how many bytes it took.
//
// The mirror of `read_into`, down to the borrow mode saying which direction the
// bytes go. A short count here is UNAMBIGUOUS in a way a short read is not — a file
// has no EOF in this direction — so there is no `at_eof`/`failed` pair to
// disambiguate it, and the number is the whole report for this call.
pub fn write_from(mut w: Writer, read buf: []u8) -> usize {
    if w.h == null { return 0 }
    if buf.len == 0 { return 0 }
    let n: usize = fwrite(buf.ptr, 1, buf.len, w.h)
    w.put = w.put + n
    return n
}

// A `str`'s bytes, exactly as they are. No encoding is applied and no terminator is
// added — `write_str(w, "\n")` is spellable, and a `write_line` would be a policy
// decision no caller in the tree has asked for yet.
pub fn write_str(mut w: Writer, read text: str) -> usize {
    if w.h == null { return 0 }
    if text.len == 0 { return 0 }
    let n: usize = fwrite(text.ptr as cptr, 1, text.len, w.h)
    w.put = w.put + n
    return n
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ progress

// Bytes this handle has handed to the stream. PROGRESS, not durability: it counts
// what `fwrite` accepted, and only `finish` says whether any of it landed.
//
// Per-handle, not per-file — an `append` writer that adds two bytes to a five-byte
// file reports 2. The module reports progress freely and reports failure at exactly
// one place, which is the line that keeps a caller from checking the wrong thing.
pub fn written(read w: Writer) -> usize {
    return w.put
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ finishing

// Close the file and report whether the bytes landed, returning the total this
// handle handed over. THE ONLY FALLIBLE FUNCTION IN THIS MODULE.
//
// Order matters and is not incidental: `ferror` is read BEFORE the close, because
// after it the FILE is gone; `fclose` then runs on every path, so nothing leaks; and
// the handle is nulled, so this binding's `Drop` finds nothing to do and a second
// `finish` on it returns `FileNotOpen` rather than closing twice.
//
// **"this binding's"** is doing real work in that sentence, and an earlier draft said
// "`Drop` can never double-close", which contradicted this same header eighty lines
// up. A `Writer` is copy-initializable: `var b: Writer = a` leaves two names for one
// `FILE*`, so `finish(b)` nulls `b` and leaves `a` holding a stale live handle that
// `a`'s `Drop` will close a second time — from ordinary, non-`unsafe` code, with no
// diagnostic. That is the missing linear-type obligation, not something this function
// can fix, and claiming otherwise would be the kind of load-bearing false comment this
// module's own tests exist to catch.
//
// The three members are actionably different, which is why this is a set and `open`'s
// single failure is not:
//
//   * `FileNotOpen`   — the handle is null: the open failed (wrong path, missing
//                       directory, interior NUL) or `finish` already ran. This member
//                       is what stops `finish` from lying — without it a `Writer`
//                       whose `create` failed would answer `ok(0)`, "fine, zero
//                       bytes", for a file that never existed.
//   * `FileWriteFailed` — the error indicator was already latched when this began, so
//                       an earlier `write_*` did not deliver and said so in its return
//                       value. An earlier failure outranks a close failure, because it
//                       is the one the caller could have caught.
//   * `FileCloseFailed` — nothing had failed before and the close, including its
//                       implicit final flush, reported a problem. THIS IS THE CASE NO
//                       PER-CALL COUNT COULD CATCH, and it is the entire reason this
//                       function exists.
//
// The last two are described MECHANICALLY on purpose, and are **asserted rather than
// tested**: `create`/`append` cannot produce an open-but-unwritable stream, so neither
// has been observed through this API or any other. A companion file that poked raw
// stdio was written to cover them and then deleted — its central assertion passed only
// because the failed write left the output buffer empty, and it reached that state by
// flushing an input stream, which is UB. A test that proves nothing and invokes UB to
// do it is worse than an acknowledged gap.
//
// Manufacturing a genuinely broken stream needs a read-only descriptor or a full
// filesystem — a portability decision, which by this module's own scope rule is
// `sys`'s. Until then this gap is stated, not papered over.
//
// The names are prefixed because the error namespace is flat and whole-program: a
// consumer declaring its own `WriteFailed` with a payload would get a diagnostic
// pointing into this module. They carry NO payload, and that is an ABI promise, not
// an omission — one payload-carrying name widens the result struct of every program
// that links this, and the payload a caller actually wants (which path) is refused by
// the escape checker anyway.
//
// The ok type is `usize` rather than `bool` because a `bool` would smuggle the
// always-answers-fine query back in through the return position.
//
// Idempotent in EFFECT (the handle is already null, so no second `fclose`, and `Drop`
// finds nothing to do) and deliberately not in REPORT: a second call is
// `err(FileNotOpen)`. A `sealed` field would be a byte spent making a caller's bug
// read more prettily.
pub fn finish(mut w: Writer) -> usize !{ FileNotOpen, FileWriteFailed, FileCloseFailed } {
    if w.h == null { return err(FileNotOpen) }
    let latched: i32 = ferror(w.h)
    let closed: i32 = fclose(w.h)
    w.h = null
    if latched != 0 { return err(FileWriteFailed) }
    if closed != 0 { return err(FileCloseFailed) }
    return ok(w.put)
}

impl Drop for Writer {
    // Bare externs only. A module-qualified call inside a trait-impl body emits the
    // module binding as if it were a local and fails in gcc (§1.6), and a `Drop` that
    // called `finish` could not report its answer anyway.
    fn drop(mut self) {
        if self.h != null { fclose(self.h) }
    }
}
